          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why is cognitive behavioural therapy described as first-line for insomnia rather than medication?",
          "answer": "According to the insomnia guidance (*Chronic insomnia: cognitive behavioral therapy as first-line treatment*, from sleep medicine and internal medicine bodies), cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia is first-line for chronic insomnia in adults. The guidance gives two reasons:\n\n- **Lasting effects:** Its benefits continue after treatment ends. The guidance says pharmacological effects generally do not.\n- **Fewer long-term harms:** It avoids the harms linked with long-term use of sleep-inducing medication.\n\nThe guidance describes CBT-I as a package of components delivered over about four to eight sessions. Two of them are:\n\n- **Stimulus control:** Using the bed only for sleep, and getting up when unable to sleep, so the bed stops signalling wakefulness.\n- **Sleep restriction:** Limiting time in bed toward actual sleep time, then extending it as sleep improves.\n\nThe retrieved passage was cut off, so it does not explain the full evidence behind these reasons or the other components. The other documents returned (on anxiety, hypertension, migraine and heart failure) do not address insomnia.\n\nWhether this approach suits a particular person is a question for a clinician who knows their situation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
